---

<DocsLayout title="Self-hosting" lead="Offhook is a backend you run on your own infrastructure: an API, a worker and Postgres, plus an optional dashboard. This page explains what each piece does. <a href='/docs/deploying/'>Deploying</a> and <a href='/docs/configuration/'>Configuration</a> cover the steps." slug="self-hosting">
        <h2 id="components">What you run</h2>
        <div class="wd-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>API</strong><span class="param-flag is-required">required</span></td>
                <td><code>python run.py</code></td>
                <td>Serves the <code>/v1</code> API, the carrier webhooks and the call audio stream. Keeps no state of its own, so you can run several behind a load balancer.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Worker</strong><span class="param-flag is-required">required</span></td>
                <td><code>python -m app.worker</code></td>
                <td>Dials queued calls and delivers webhooks. Without it, calls stay <code>queued</code>. You can run several; each job is claimed by exactly one worker.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Postgres</strong><span class="param-flag is-required">required</span></td>
                <td><code>postgres:16</code></td>
                <td>The only datastore. Tenants, encrypted connections, agents, calls, the job queue, usage and the event log all live here. No Redis or message broker needed.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Dashboard</strong><span class="param-flag">optional</span></td>
                <td>static site in <code>dashboard/</code></td>
                <td>A web console for your calls, agents and keys. Build it to static files and host it anywhere. Sign-in uses Clerk.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <Callout kind="note">
          The API and the worker are separate processes. Each reads its configuration when it
          starts, and they share only the database. When you change configuration, restart
          both.
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="architecture">How a call flows</h2>
        <p>Calls are asynchronous, which is why the worker is required:</p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "Flow", lang: "text", code: `client ──POST /v1/calls──▶  API  ──enqueue──▶  Postgres (call_jobs)
                                                   │
                            worker ──claim job────▶─┘
                              │
                              ├─ decrypt the tenant's connection secrets
                              ├─ dial through the carrier (Plivo or Twilio)
                              └─ carrier answers ──▶ webhook to PUBLIC_BASE_URL
                                                       │
                          API audio stream (WebSocket) ◀┘
                              │
                              └─ STT ─▶ LLM ─▶ TTS  (one pipeline per call)` }]} />
        <p>
          This has two consequences for your setup. The worker needs outbound internet access
          to reach the carrier and the AI providers. And <code>PUBLIC_BASE_URL</code> must be an
          HTTPS address the carrier can reach from the internet, because the carrier calls back
          into the API when the call is answered.
        </p>

        <h2 id="prerequisites">What you need</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Python 3.13</strong> where the API and worker run, or one container image for both.</li>
          <li><strong>Postgres</strong> that both can reach. The project is tested with Postgres 16.</li>
          <li><strong>A master encryption key</strong>: 32 random bytes, base64-encoded, kept outside the database. If you lose it, every stored provider key is unrecoverable. Keep it in a secrets manager, or use AWS KMS.</li>
          <li><strong>A public HTTPS address</strong> for the API, if you place real phone calls.</li>
          <li><strong>Accounts with your providers</strong>: Deepgram, Cartesia, a language model and a carrier. Their keys go into each tenant's <a href="/docs/connections/">connections</a>, not into server configuration.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Ready? Continue to <a href="/docs/deploying/">Deploying</a>.</p>

</DocsLayout>
